  "christmas",
  "folk"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 60,
 "key": "Fm",
 "timeSignature": "2/4",
 "pickupBeats": 0.5,
 "credits": {
  "words": "traditional Catalan, as printed by Lluís Millet (1917)",
  "tune": "traditional Catalan, as printed by Lluís Millet (1917)",
  "arrangement": "Lluís Millet (1867-1941), harmony printed 1917, adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: a traditional Catalan carol printed with no author named in Lluís Millet's Pel nostre ideal (Barcelona, 1917); Millet died 1941",
  "tune": "public domain: a traditional Catalan melody, harmonised by Lluís Millet (died 1941) and printed in 1917",
  "arrangement": "Lluís Millet's harmony for solo voice and humming men's choir (public domain: Millet died 1941), adapted for beginners and written for Stackapella a tone below the print. Bars are counted from the first full bar (bars 1 to 3 the choir's opening, 4 to 32 the verse) and notes named in G minor as printed, the tenors' treble staves an octave lower as sung. Millet's four choir parts (Tenors I and II, Baritons, Baixos) become three hummed parts, each humming \"oo\" as his choir hums with closed mouth. The bass is Millet's bass, except: his drops from G to the G below in bars 2, 5 and 6 stay on the upper G, a pedal through bars 1 to 7; his repeated notes are tied for the hum (pickup and bar 1, bars 9, 13, 14, 16, 20, 23, 24, 26, 30); the quaver C at the end of bar 11 is left out for the breath; bars 19 and 29 are D then A (for D, D) so the bass does not move in octaves with the tune into bars 20 and 30; and the first verse's bar 32 is on the upper G. The alto is Millet's first tenors, except where they would double the tune an octave down or leave the duet without the chord's third: bars 5 and 8 begin on the baritones' B-flat, bar 9 is the baritones' A then the second tenors' C, bar 10 is the second tenors' D then the first tenors' F-sharp, bars 12 and 22 are the first tenors' F through the bar (for G, F; the second tenors' D would double the tune an octave down), bars 13 and 23 are the second tenors' E-flat then the first tenors' F, bars 17 and 27 are F then E-flat (the baritones' notes an octave up, for the first tenors' D and C, and bar 27's F, A, F, E-flat), bar 28 is C as in bar 18 (for D), bar 29 holds the second tenors' C, bars 21, 30 and the first verse's bar 31 are the second tenors' B-flat and C; the first tenors' quaver and semiquaver neighbour notes are left out in bars 5, 6, 14, 15, 19, 20, 24, 25, so the alto moves mostly by step. The first tenors' note at the start of bar 11, tied from bar 10's F-sharp, is sung F-sharp. The tenor takes Millet's second tenors or baritones, whichever lies under the alto: the second tenors in bars 1 to 3, 14 to 16, 18 to 20, 24 to 26 and the first verse's bar 32; the baritones in bars 4, 6, 7, 12, 22, 29 and verse 2's bars 30 and 31; bar 5 is G then A (for the baritones' B-flat, A, F-sharp), bars 8 and 9 begin on the basses' F, bars 10 and 11 hold the tenors' A with the baritones' F-sharp on bar 11's second beat, bars 13 and 23 are the baritones' G then the second tenors' B natural, bars 15 and 25 hold the second tenors' C, bars 17 and 27 are the second tenors' B-flat and G (their C left out), bar 28 is G as in bar 18, bar 21 and the first verse's bars 30 and 31 hold the baritones' G. The choir's breath commas (bars 7, 11, 15, 18, 21, 25 and 28) are sung as quaver rests taken from the note before; the commas in bar 31 are left out. Dynamics, accents, staccato and portato marks are not written in. The ending: the print ends each verse with the solo resting while the choir holds a G minor chord under a pause (bar 32), then gives the next verse's pickup on a lone quaver after the double bar marked FI; here the first verse's bar 32 lasts a beat and a half, without the pause, and verse 2's pickup takes its last quaver, so the song keeps whole 2/4 bars; verse 2 ends on bar 31, the choir holding Millet's closing G minor chord there (alto B-flat, tenor D, bass G) under the soprano's held G; the alto in bar 19 holds C (for the first tenors' A then C), avoiding a bare fourth in the duet; repeated notes are tied for the hum in the alto and tenor as in the bass.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "Lluís Millet (died 1941), Pel nostre ideal: recull d'escrits de Lluís Millet, director de l'Orfeó Català, preface by Joan Llongueres (died 1953) (Barcelona, 1917; no publisher named on the title page), Exemple XIV, \"El cant dels aucells\", headed \"Harm. de'n Lluís Millet\", pp. 237 to 241 (the music on pp. 237 to 240, the full text under it on pp. 240 and 241), for solo (treble clef, words underlaid) and men's choir (Tenors I, Tenors II, Baritons, Baixos) humming (\"Boca tancada\"), Moderat, 2/4, two flats, in G minor, sung here a tone lower, in F minor; read from the scan of the Arxiu Municipal del Masnou's copy, archive.org/details/pel-nostre-ideal-lluis-millet/page/n237 (leaves n237 to n241). A footnote on p. 241 says the score and parts were published by Iberia Musical (not seen). Every note of the tune and the choir as printed, transposed, except as listed under arrangement and these: the tune's grace notes before \"tant\" (bar 24) and \"dro-\" (bar 29) left out; breaths written in for beginners by shortening a held note of the tune: \"nar\" (a dotted crotchet sung as a crotchet), the first \"sa,\" (bar 11, three beats sung as two and a half) and each \"van\" (a dotted crotchet sung as a crotchet, four times), and the last \"a.\" held three and a half beats to the final chord. Words: verse 1 is the stanza printed under the solo, \"Al veure despuntar\", spelled as in the full text printed under the music on p. 240 (\"més\", where the underlay has \"mes\"), with the underlay's word division (\"dit-xo-sa\", \"au-cel-lets\", \"fes-te-jar-lo\"); verse 2 is the text's second stanza, \"L'Aliga imperial\", underlaid by us to verse 1's notes, \"L'Aliga\" sung L'A-li-ga and \"imperial\" as three syllables, im-pe-rial; the print's dialogue dashes (after \"dihent:\" and after \"alegría.\") are left out; otherwise as printed, old spelling and accents kept (\"lo\", \"ab\", \"aucellets\", \"dihent\", \"tráurens\", \"darnos\", \"melodía\", \"alegría\"). The print's underlay gives the pickup after the double bar as \"L'au-\", which matches no stanza printed with it; it is read as a misprint and sung \"L'A-\". As in the print, the last three lines of each stanza are sung twice. Checked against Obra del Cançoner Popular de Catalunya, Materials, vol. I (Barcelona, 1926), p. 380, archive.org/details/obradelcanconerpopularmaterials1/page/n391, which prints the same tune and rhythm in G minor (crotchet = 60, the tempo used here) as \"noté et harmonisé pour soprano solo et choeur d'hommes par Lluis Millet\", and against Cançoner popular (Barcelona, 1903), Cançó I, archive.org/details/canonerpopular00barcuoft/page/n12, the same tune and form in another key"
 },
